<bp:ProteinReference rdf:ID="ProteinReference4526">
 <bp:xref rdf:resource="#UnificationXref50610" />
 <bp:organism rdf:resource="#BioSource1" />
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">KIAA1238</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">FAM80B</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">UniProt:Q9ULI2 RIMKLB</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">RIMKLB</bp:name>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">FUNCTION Catalyzes the synthesis of beta-citryl-L-glutamate and N-acetyl-L-aspartyl-L-glutamate. Beta-citryl-L-glutamate is synthesized more efficiently than N-acetyl-L-aspartyl-L-glutamate.CATALYTIC ACTIVITY citrate + L-glutamate + ATP = beta-citrylglutamate + ADP + phosphate + H(+)CATALYTIC ACTIVITY N-acetyl-L-aspartate + L-glutamate + ATP = N-acetyl-L-aspartyl-L-glutamate + ADP + phosphate + H(+)COFACTOR Binds 2 magnesium or manganese ions per subunit.SUBCELLULAR LOCATION N-acetyl-L-aspartyl-L-glutamate (NAAG) is the most abundant dipeptide present in vertebrate central nervous system (CNS). Beta-citryl-L-glutamate, a structural analog of NAAG, is present in testis and immature brain.MISCELLANEOUS May be produced at very low levels due to a premature stop codon in the mRNA, leading to nonsense-mediated mRNA decay.SIMILARITY Belongs to the RimK family.SEQUENCE CAUTION Extended N-terminus.</bp:comment>
</bp:ProteinReference>

