 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">glutamate + NAD(P)+ =&gt; alpha-ketoglutarate + NH4+ + NAD(P)H + H+ (GLUD1,2)</bp:name>
 <bp:name rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">H2O + L-glutamate + NADP(+) =&gt; 2-oxoglutarate + H(+) + NADPH + NH4(+)</bp:name>
 <bp:comment rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Mitochondrial glutamate dehydrogenase 1 (GLUD1) catalyzes the reversible reaction of glutamate and NAD(P)+ to form 2-oxoglutarate, NAD(P)H + H+, and ammonia (Fang et al. 2002). Mature GLUD1 protein lacks the 53 aminoterminal residues of the nascent protein (Julliard and Smith 1979), which function as a mitochondrial import signal. The active form of the enzyme is a hexamer, allosterically activated by ADP and inhibited by GTP (Fang et al. 2002; Smith et al. 2002). GLUD2 is inferred to catalyze the same reaction based on its structural similarity to GLUD1.</bp:comment>
